1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:10,700
This is no joke, Okay, this is true.

3
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:16,920
Years ago, I taught principles of navigation for the Department of Natural Resources, for the hunter education class,

4
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:19,780
because we were picking up a lot of lost hunters every year,

5
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:26,720
And so they developed this questionnaire where they would ask these lost hunters a battery of different questions,

6
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:30,900
And one of the questions they asked them was: do you carry a compass?

7
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:33,680
And most of them did.

8
00:00:34,519 --> 00:00:41,680
And so then the next question was: well, you know, why didn't you use the compass to, you know, find your way out of the woods?

9
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:46,480
And the answer was: well, you know, I tried, but I don't know how.

10
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:51,820
And then the next question was: well, if you don't know how, then why do you carry a compass?

11
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:56,780
And the answer- believe it or not- was: everyone should carry a compass in case they get lost.

12
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:02,060
So I don't know if that's a joke or not, but it's a true story.

13
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:08,740
This is Cliff Jacobson, and you're listening to the Scoutmaster Podcast with Clark Green.

14
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:11,720
Hey, this is podcast number 54..

15
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:12,320
Hey,

16
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:42,740
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

17
00:01:42,979 --> 00:01:44,199
This is Clark Green.

18
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:53,340
So most of this podcast is an interview that I recently conducted with guide and author Cliff Jacobson, who's kind of one of my outdoor heroes.

19
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:58,900
There are a couple of his books that I keep very close to hand and I refer to very often.

20
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:02,620
We'll hear a little bit more about those in the interview.

21
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,200
Let me catch up with some correspondence here.

22
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:07,800
Mr Merritt writes: I love your podcast.

23
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:10,180
You're actually interesting to listen to.

24
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:15,500
You have great thoughts and it really helps me develop my troop's youth leadership.

25
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:17,120
Well, thank you, Mr Mayor.

26
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:19,920
I'm interesting to listen to.

27
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:24,000
You know we could take that in a lot of different ways.

28
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:26,660
I'll take it as a compliment.

29
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:32,020
Bill says this. I'm slowly working through the podcast archive and I'm now on number 42..

30
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:34,900
I am really enjoying your comments and suggestions.

31
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:36,140
Well, thanks, Bill.

32
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:37,780
Thanks for being in touch.

33
00:02:37,780 --> 00:02:49,420
If you want to work your way through the archive along with Bill, if you go to scoutmastertypepadcom, you'll find a link right underneath the banner to the podcast archive.

34
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:58,360
You can work your way through too, and maybe we'll start a support group or something like that for the poor people who are trying to do that.

35
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:05,140
Here's a couple of comments from our discussion of Venture Patrols on Podcast 53, our last podcast,

36
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:11,200
Mike said this. he said that's an excellent solution for boys that want more high adventure in the troop.

37
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:13,899
I plan to incorporate the Venture Patrol in our troops soon.

38
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:24,399
Alan says this. the chart of the Explorer crew that you linked to on last week's podcast reminds me of what we did a few years ago when we put together a Philmont trip.

39
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:30,220
On alternate weekends the crew that we put together did training, hikes and went on treks.

40
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:34,260
Otherwise they were just a normal part of their troop and patrol.

41
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:36,020
The Philmont trip was over,

42
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:41,560
The scouts went back to their troop patrols and in a few cases, to a nearby Venture Crew.

43
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:46,280
In time most of these guys kind of gravitated towards the Venture Crew.

44
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:51,700
We didn't think about appointing a Venture Patrol leader who would replicate the way the Philmont crew operated.

45
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:56,260
I wondered, if we did this, would we have kept older boys closer to the troop?

46
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:59,260
Well, Alan, I've got a couple of thoughts on that.

47
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:01,860
It's good for guys to stay in scouting,

48
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:05,880
And if the way they're going to stay in scouting is to go to a Venture Crew, more power to them-

49
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:06,880
I think it's great.

50
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:12,780
I have found that the Venture Patrol concept does keep guys close to the troop.

51
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:15,840
I mean, that's kind of a fringe benefit of it

52
00:04:16,019 --> 00:04:23,240
And it lets them do the things that they really want to go and do away from the quote-unquote little guys.

53
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:26,080
So here's another aspect of that.

54
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:27,520
Steve wrote in and said this:

55
00:04:27,980 --> 00:04:32,220
Our troop's recently retired Scoutmaster has just finished creating a Venture Crew-

56
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:34,520
All the charter paperwork and everything-

57
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:36,740
And it's associated with our troop.

58
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:39,460
The scouts are easy to get started with the Ventures

59
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:44,080
And they've been eager to do that long before the crew became official,

60
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:49,280
As a matter of fact, they started planning special Venture activities they could do on normal troop outings

61
00:04:49,719 --> 00:04:57,440
While they had a great time. it also created this kind of I'm not in your patrol, I'm a Venture attitude that prevailed throughout the campout.

62
00:04:57,960 --> 00:04:59,020
Now how do you handle that?

63
00:04:59,359 --> 00:05:02,840
How do you keep a virtual Venture Patrol virtual?

64
00:05:02,840 --> 00:05:16,679
Without going into a really incredibly long explanation on this, Steve, the older boy program in scouting is something that is much discussed, much fretted over.

65
00:05:17,239 --> 00:05:19,099
Lots of people talk about it.

66
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:28,000
You know our summer camps are very sensitive to offering these older scout opportunities so that boys just don't leave scouting in droves.

67
00:05:28,679 --> 00:05:31,719
Here's my basic thinking on it.

68
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:35,600
But the older boy program in scouting is really easy.

69
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:37,620
They lead their troop.

70
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:46,460
Now this leads to the common complaints of: well, I don't want to have to pal around with these younger kids and blah blah. you know,

71
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:48,780
I understand that

72
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:50,200
And that is a real concern.

73
00:05:50,200 --> 00:06:00,240
But while it might take some time to do, when you create a culture of responsibility and leadership amongst your older scouts, they're not going anywhere.

74
00:06:00,979 --> 00:06:02,680
They will take to the job,

75
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:03,740
They'll take to the challenges,

76
00:06:03,900 --> 00:06:04,599
They will love it

77
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:09,780
And they will stay with you all the way up through their graduation from high school

78
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:13,080
And they'll become key leaders within your troop.

79
00:06:14,159 --> 00:06:19,700
So just a bit of advice there and kind of what the thinking behind it is.

80
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:26,960
Well, before we get too far into the podcast, I want to get into our interview with Cliff Jacobson.

81
00:06:27,219 --> 00:06:32,539
There's a lot of links on the blog to books that we mention and to Cliff's website

82
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:37,700
And, like I said, I was just really excited to be able to spend some time and to speak with him.

83
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:42,800
And after the interview, well, I'll go over how you can get in touch with me through email and online

84
00:06:42,980 --> 00:06:45,599
Without further ado. let's get started, shall we?

85
00:06:45,599 --> 00:06:45,700
Let's get started,

86
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:46,099
Let's get started,

87
00:06:46,099 --> 00:06:46,700
Let's get started,

88
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:47,700
Let's get started,

89
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:49,680
Let's get started.

90
00:06:49,680 --> 00:07:14,100
Today I'm speaking with wilderness guide Cliff Jacobson, who's the author of over a dozen top-selling books on camping and canoeing.

91
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:18,860
Cliff's an environmental science teacher who retired after 34 years of teaching

92
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:24,660
And he's the 2003 recipient of the Legends of Paddling Award from the American Canoe Association.

93
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:31,180
As a distinguished Eagle Scout, Cliff continues to contribute to scouting through his articles in Scouting Magazine

94
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:37,300
And, as a matter of fact, if you look in the most current edition- that's the January- February 2011 edition-

95
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:40,840
you're going to find an article by Cliff about building fires in the rain,

96
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:44,460
And Cliff joins me today from his home in River Falls, Wisconsin.

97
00:07:44,620 --> 00:07:45,400
Cliff, how are you doing?

98
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:47,239
Hey, yeah, listen, man,

99
00:07:47,320 --> 00:07:48,479
Listen, I'm doing great here.

100
00:07:48,620 --> 00:07:51,340
Listen, I appreciate the nice intro,

101
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:56,799
But I just got to say something about. thank you for mentioning the latest issue of Scouting Magazine.

102
00:07:56,800 --> 00:08:04,060
But for your listeners, I think that particular article is a very interesting and unusual one,

103
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:06,200
And why?

104
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:12,040
Because if you look at the picture in there and you read it, it talks about an axe,

105
00:08:12,540 --> 00:08:12,780
Right?

106
00:08:12,820 --> 00:08:17,000
You know how traditionally the scouts have felt about axes,

107
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:30,100
And it took a lot of convincing for me to get the boys down in Texas to agree that it was important to be able to build a fire,

108
00:08:30,220 --> 00:08:32,159
to be able to use an axe in that manner,

109
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:32,740
Right,

110
00:08:32,860 --> 00:08:36,520
And so I think that's a real plus.

111
00:08:37,159 --> 00:08:38,039
I think so too,

112
00:08:38,679 --> 00:08:38,820
Yeah,

113
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:43,039
And I think scouts who embrace that method will find that it's perfectly safe

114
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:45,580
and you'll always be able to get a fire going for your boys.

115
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:50,560
Well, you know, a lot of times people look at a teenage boy and an axe

116
00:08:50,660 --> 00:08:53,520
and they put those two things together and it spells catastrophe.

117
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:55,760
But that ain't necessarily so.

118
00:08:55,860 --> 00:08:56,760
We both know that,

119
00:08:57,280 --> 00:08:57,620
Yeah,

120
00:08:57,840 --> 00:09:00,220
But anyway, I just thought I would mention it.

121
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:00,840
But, thank you,

122
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:03,460
I'm really curious about your time in Scouting.

123
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:07,280
Tell me a little bit about where you were Scouting and where you grew up.

124
00:09:07,620 --> 00:09:11,060
Well, actually I grew up and I was born in Chicago-

125
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:12,800
Oh, no, kidding,

126
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:13,500
Yeah, yeah,

127
00:09:13,599 --> 00:09:17,880
And I lived right in the heart of Chicago in a third-story apartment building,

128
00:09:18,099 --> 00:09:19,680
and I hated every minute of it.

129
00:09:20,140 --> 00:09:25,420
As long as I can remember, I had in my blood this idea that I wanted to go camping,

130
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:28,660
that I wanted to be outside, and I wanted to be outdoors camping,

131
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:30,859
And Chicago didn't have it,

132
00:09:30,860 --> 00:09:33,520
And the thing that did was the Boy Scouts.

133
00:09:34,220 --> 00:09:40,360
And so I embraced the Boy Scouts with full gusto and I belonged to one of those troops,

134
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:41,100
Troop 21..

135
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:42,700
I still remember Troop 21.

136
00:09:43,380 --> 00:09:46,460
And we went camping once a month, rain or shine,

137
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:49,800
And that was absolutely the highlight of my life.

138
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:55,500
And the greatest compliment that anybody ever gave me was a Scoutmaster once said to me.

139
00:09:55,640 --> 00:09:59,380
he said, Cliff, he says I've read your books. he said,

140
00:09:59,439 --> 00:10:01,560
and I don't know who you think you're kidding.

141
00:10:01,699 --> 00:10:06,099
He says, all you've done is taken all that old Boy Scout stuff and modernized it.

142
00:10:06,339 --> 00:10:09,300
And I said, shh, promise me, you won't tell a soul,

143
00:10:10,040 --> 00:10:12,339
You know. so yeah, I owe a lot to Scouting.

144
00:10:13,160 --> 00:10:17,500
What do you think kept you involved and interested in Scouts as much as you were?

145
00:10:17,940 --> 00:10:19,460
Oh, it was the camping period.

146
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:21,600
It was totally the camping.

147
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:27,220
And you know, we used to have a saying, and I think it's most Scouters still remember:

148
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:30,380
you take the outing out of Scouting, you take the Scouting out of the Boy.

149
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:31,260
Is that still a saying?

150
00:10:31,720 --> 00:10:32,880
Yeah, it's still out there.

151
00:10:33,320 --> 00:10:33,600
Yeah,

152
00:10:33,900 --> 00:10:35,940
That to me is a major thing.

153
00:10:35,940 --> 00:10:42,320
I mean because you know, face it, nobody is taking kids in the outdoors.

154
00:10:42,340 --> 00:10:44,100
Now, just think about that.

155
00:10:44,540 --> 00:10:50,800
Scouting is literally the last place on earth that takes kids out camping.

156
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:56,800
I mean, sure, there's some summer camps, but that's a once-a-year shot for a week or so

157
00:10:56,940 --> 00:10:58,460
and then you're done with Scouting.

158
00:10:58,640 --> 00:10:59,280
It's a lifestyle.

159
00:11:00,220 --> 00:11:01,700
So you know, don't minimize it.

160
00:11:01,700 --> 00:11:07,960
I would say to Scout leaders: boy, don't minimize the importance of taking kids on camping trips.

161
00:11:07,960 --> 00:11:12,540
I would say that's the most important thing you can do is take them out on camping trips.

162
00:11:12,900 --> 00:11:19,580
So when you were a Scout, I mean you didn't go and scale mountains or run rivers or anything like that,

163
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:20,520
You just went out camping.

164
00:11:20,660 --> 00:11:21,820
Yeah, we went out camping,

165
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:25,960
We would just go out in the woods. that we used to have in those days.

166
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:29,900
Now this would be considered blasphemy today, but it was so fun when I was a kid.

167
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:34,920
Now, imagine a group of 14-year-olds- 13, 14-year-olds- going on a rifle hike.

168
00:11:35,020 --> 00:11:40,819
We would take our .22 rifles and we'd go in the woods with our leaders and we'd just shoot at stuff,

169
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:43,699
We'd just plink, you know,

170
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:47,079
And we would have a couple of rifle hikes a year.

171
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:51,100
And there's no way you could do anything like that today.

172
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:52,000
I'm afraid not.

173
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,740
But I have vivid memories of the rifle hike.

174
00:11:54,880 --> 00:11:56,880
You weren't too worried about where you went.

175
00:11:57,180 --> 00:12:04,780
You weren't too worried about some kind of- you know, excellent program or diversion or something like that.

176
00:12:04,900 --> 00:12:07,320
What you wanted to do was get out and sleep in a tent.

177
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:09,700
Yeah, that's exactly it.

178
00:12:09,940 --> 00:12:12,180
And I mean people talk about going light today.

179
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:13,180
Are you kidding me?

180
00:12:13,540 --> 00:12:14,800
I mean we went light

181
00:12:15,220 --> 00:12:19,280
When we would hike. we'd do 20-mile hikes when I was like 13 years old,

182
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:23,760
And I think if my pack weighed 15 pounds, that was probably a lot

183
00:12:24,020 --> 00:12:25,319
And it did everything I needed.

184
00:12:25,819 --> 00:12:31,720
You know, it had my sleeping bag, my poncho, a little cook kit thing and a knife,

185
00:12:32,180 --> 00:12:33,920
And I had a little hatchet. then,

186
00:12:34,079 --> 00:12:34,260
Right,

187
00:12:34,319 --> 00:12:38,220
And I think, you know, maybe I had a sweater or something like that.

188
00:12:38,339 --> 00:12:39,660
That was pretty much it.

189
00:12:40,319 --> 00:12:41,699
You know, you built a fire,

190
00:12:41,819 --> 00:12:43,020
You put up your poncho,

191
00:12:43,380 --> 00:12:48,380
And if it rained, well, you just learned how to rig a tarp so that you didn't get wet.

192
00:12:48,380 --> 00:12:50,400
And if you did get wet, well, it was part of the game.

193
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:51,840
And you know, we had a lot of fun.

194
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:54,120
Well, and that's why boys join scouting,

195
00:12:54,340 --> 00:12:54,620
Right,

196
00:12:54,620 --> 00:12:55,820
Because that's what they want to do,

197
00:12:56,140 --> 00:12:56,540
Exactly.

198
00:12:56,860 --> 00:12:59,560
So you went on to become an Eagle Scout.

199
00:13:00,540 --> 00:13:00,860
Yeah,

200
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:03,380
Do I have the date, the year right? 1956?

201
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:06,740
Yeah, that's right, 1956, yeah,

202
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:10,840
And what do you remember about that whole process putting that all together?

203
00:13:10,840 --> 00:13:13,380
Well, you know, I was actually thinking about that.

204
00:13:13,640 --> 00:13:15,520
I remember, of course, it was 21: merit badges.

205
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:16,740
Is it still 21?

206
00:13:16,740 --> 00:13:17,860
It's 21,. yep,

207
00:13:17,860 --> 00:13:18,800
Bill 21..

208
00:13:19,380 --> 00:13:24,160
I vividly remember flunking life-saving three times.

209
00:13:24,580 --> 00:13:25,280
Three times.

210
00:13:25,780 --> 00:13:27,080
That was at summer camp,

211
00:13:27,260 --> 00:13:30,960
And so it took me two years to get it, to get life-saving merit badges.

212
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:32,520
You had to have life-saving.

213
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:35,720
There was no way getting out of swimming and life-saving.

214
00:13:35,840 --> 00:13:39,380
If you couldn't swim, you couldn't get your Eagle. in those days

215
00:13:39,460 --> 00:13:41,180
They didn't have optional badges.

216
00:13:41,180 --> 00:13:45,680
I think it's good now that we do for people who can't.

217
00:13:45,920 --> 00:13:48,099
I don't remember a service project

218
00:13:48,599 --> 00:13:49,939
Now. maybe I did one,

219
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:50,780
I don't remember it.

220
00:13:50,780 --> 00:13:53,280
I don't remember in 1956 if we had one or not.

221
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:58,640
There's a lot of guys who would know who will let me know, who are listening to this.

222
00:13:58,760 --> 00:13:59,780
Yeah, that would be interesting.

223
00:13:59,900 --> 00:14:02,660
You know, maybe I had one, did one, but it was so long ago.

224
00:14:02,660 --> 00:14:07,920
Oh, and I also remember the boys were generally older when they got their Eagles in.

225
00:14:07,980 --> 00:14:10,959
There were very few Eagles before 15..

226
00:14:11,319 --> 00:14:12,140
I was 16..

227
00:14:12,620 --> 00:14:14,660
That was a pretty average age then.

228
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:16,380
That's still my experience today.

229
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:18,199
16 actually is a little young,

230
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:22,699
At least in my experience. they end up dragging their feet until they're 17 and a half.

231
00:14:22,699 --> 00:14:30,839
Well, that's okay too, because I think you get a lot more experience as you get older,

232
00:14:30,840 --> 00:14:30,920
Right?

233
00:14:31,700 --> 00:14:34,560
