00:24:04,500 --> 00:24:07,220
And all of a sudden I realized I didn't know where I was,

422
00:24:07,220 --> 00:24:09,120
I didn't know what was going on,

423
00:24:09,340 --> 00:24:10,220
Totally lost,

424
00:24:10,220 --> 00:24:16,420
And I what I had. I had my silver compass and I had some water and uh, make a sandwich.

425
00:24:16,420 --> 00:24:21,000
And and I said I know, the only way out of here is go due west and I will eventually

426
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:22,820
hit highway one-on-one in the ocean.

427
00:24:23,400 --> 00:24:24,840
And that's what I did.

428
00:24:24,900 --> 00:24:30,080
I walked for three days and on the third day, uh, morning of the third day, came out

429
00:24:30,080 --> 00:24:31,840
to a little town called remote Oregon-

430
00:24:32,120 --> 00:24:33,320
That's the name of the town-

431
00:24:33,320 --> 00:24:38,139
And I was picked up by a um, uh, trucker on a log truck.

432
00:24:38,220 --> 00:24:45,840
It took me back to my car and I drove home and, uh, I never told a soul, because foresters

433
00:24:45,840 --> 00:24:46,560
don't get lost.

434
00:24:46,560 --> 00:24:53,399
But the but the point, the point is, is the way I survived. that was simply because, one,

435
00:24:53,460 --> 00:24:54,399
I had a compass.

436
00:24:55,040 --> 00:24:57,939
Two, I knew if I headed west I would hit the ocean.

437
00:24:57,940 --> 00:25:03,420
And three, I had what what they call a survival PMA, or positive mental latitude.

438
00:25:03,620 --> 00:25:07,880
And another interesting point I want to make: you know when I started all this out, we didn't

439
00:25:07,880 --> 00:25:10,280
have satellite phones and GPS units

440
00:25:11,220 --> 00:25:14,980
And- and these wilderness trips were different than you- had to be really careful

