00:26:39,760 --> 00:26:44,420
have been done differently or a different approach or where things kind of went south on somebody.

464
00:26:44,580 --> 00:26:45,500
That's a good idea.

465
00:26:45,500 --> 00:26:51,100
You know, I'm very interested in becoming a good judge of what's smart and what's not.

466
00:26:51,400 --> 00:26:54,420
you know how to keep people safe and alive when you're out there,

467
00:26:55,680 --> 00:26:59,600
And that's one of the things I really value about your writing too is there's a lot of

468
00:27:00,180 --> 00:27:02,520
that's just in the background. all the time

469
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:06,780
They've done. they do that for whitewater accidents, uh, for what? people who you know

470
00:27:07,160 --> 00:27:10,500
are drowning, and things like that in, uh, in whitewater.

471
00:27:10,500 --> 00:27:12,400
So, yeah, I think that would be a really good idea.

472
00:27:12,520 --> 00:27:16,600
You've led some pretty amazing expeditions up into Northern Canada.

473
00:27:17,300 --> 00:27:20,880
Uh, is there anyone that stands out as being the most memorable for you?

474
00:27:21,400 --> 00:27:21,800
Oh, yeah,

475
00:27:22,460 --> 00:27:22,860
Yeah,

476
00:27:22,860 --> 00:27:23,880
The Hood River, of course.

477
00:27:23,980 --> 00:27:25,560
I mean, that's where Susie and I got married,

478
00:27:25,680 --> 00:27:26,400
The second Hood River.

479
00:27:26,520 --> 00:27:27,560
Tell me a little bit about that.

480
00:27:27,720 --> 00:27:32,920
Uh, the wedding story is actually an expedition canoeing, but the Hood. the Hood is a is a

481
00:27:32,920 --> 00:27:40,040
magical river because it combines, you know, muskox, caribou, uh, grizzly bears

482
00:27:40,040 --> 00:27:42,340
With thundering falls, huge falls.

483
00:27:42,500 --> 00:27:47,820
I mean Wilberforce Falls, where we were married- drops 160 feet through a three-mile canyon.

484
00:27:48,060 --> 00:27:50,840
I mean US Niagara: drops 167 feet,

485
00:27:51,159 --> 00:27:52,460
So you can see the magnitude

486
00:27:52,899 --> 00:27:56,560
And that's like the third highest falls in North America, I believe.

487
00:27:56,980 --> 00:28:01,180
Well, that was a. that was a magical trip because, you know, Susie and I got married on it.

488
00:28:01,480 --> 00:28:07,879
Uh, the other trip that was most memorable to me was, uh, the North Knife River in um,

489
00:28:08,220 --> 00:28:09,720
Manitoba floats into Hudson Bay.

490
00:28:09,720 --> 00:28:13,000
I've actually done the North Knife, uh three times or four times,

491
00:28:13,060 --> 00:28:13,680
I can't remember.

492
00:28:14,160 --> 00:28:19,840
But on the last trip, um, we were chased, uh, we were chased downriver by a polar bear.

493
00:28:19,980 --> 00:28:23,460
He came on. a polar bear came, uh, came on land after us.

494
00:28:23,680 --> 00:28:25,840
We had a fire, a shot below his nose.

495
00:28:26,320 --> 00:28:28,320
Uh, that was a pretty scary thing.

496
00:28:28,420 --> 00:28:32,660
I mean, we saw. and then one of the trips on the North Knife, we saw it was saw 11

497
00:28:32,660 --> 00:28:33,260
polar bears.

498
00:28:33,260 --> 00:28:39,780
Um, so, uh, I would say I would say those two, the hood and the North Knife, are two of

499
00:28:39,780 --> 00:28:43,900
my most memorable rivers. but you know, every river is memorable,

500
00:28:44,320 --> 00:28:45,920
They're, all of them are wonderful.

501
00:28:46,340 --> 00:28:49,360
You know, I sometimes I go to the Boundary Waters and I love that.

502
00:28:49,460 --> 00:28:50,300
You know, I love that too.

503
00:28:50,340 --> 00:28:55,580
The Boundary Waters isn't as isn't as adventurous as Northern Canada, but it's much more relaxing.

504
00:28:55,580 --> 00:29:00,860
So I don't know, they're a lot, they're all. they all begin to flow together after a while.

505
00:29:00,980 --> 00:29:05,260
The Hood River just kind of put a pin in the map on that for me.

506
00:29:05,480 --> 00:29:11,120
Uh, you have to go first of all to the Northwest Territories of Canada and then you go to Yellowknife,

507
00:29:11,199 --> 00:29:14,800
which would be- is a capital of the Northwest Territories,

508
00:29:15,300 --> 00:29:21,980
Almost new north. roughly. you'll go to, uh, Bathurst Inlet on the Arctic Ocean, and that's

509
00:29:21,980 --> 00:29:23,399
where the Hood River ends.

510
00:29:23,400 --> 00:29:26,140
It ends at Bathurst Inlet on on the Arctic Ocean.

511
00:29:26,400 --> 00:29:27,480
So you are out there.

512
00:29:27,500 --> 00:29:28,680
Yeah, you're above the Arctic Circle.

513
00:29:28,960 --> 00:29:34,460
Um, we did a river in the Yukon called the Snake. uh, the Snake River's above the Arctic Circle too.

514
00:29:34,520 --> 00:29:36,000
That's even further north in the Hood.

515
00:29:36,520 --> 00:29:42,420
There's something about the province of Nunavut, Northwest Territories, and the new province of Nunavut

516
00:29:42,800 --> 00:29:44,400
that I just love. the topography,

517
00:29:45,240 --> 00:29:45,820
I don't know why.

518
00:29:46,080 --> 00:29:47,520
The wildlife is incredible.

519
00:29:47,740 --> 00:29:50,600
I mean tens of thousands, hundreds of thousands of caribou.

520
00:29:50,600 --> 00:29:57,740
And you know, when caribou decide they want to cross a body of water, the alpha male looks around, looks around.

521
00:29:57,840 --> 00:30:02,679
When he decides to go, he jumps in and then they just all blindly follow.

522
00:30:02,680 --> 00:30:09,760
So we're coming around a bend and we're sort of single-filing along the left edge because there's a rapid ahead.

523
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:12,880
The caribou are on this hilltop right to our left,

524
00:30:13,120 --> 00:30:14,040
Thousands of them.

525
00:30:14,520 --> 00:30:17,320
The alpha male comes over and he looks down into the water.

526
00:30:17,580 --> 00:30:20,280
He doesn't see the canoes because we're too close to the bank.

527
00:30:20,280 --> 00:30:27,940
And then he jumps and when he jumps his hooves miss Susie's head by just a foot or so.

528
00:30:28,100 --> 00:30:29,600
I can feel the air,

529
00:30:29,740 --> 00:30:30,340
I mean the back.

530
00:30:30,420 --> 00:30:32,860
I can feel the wind from his body hit me in the face.

531
00:30:33,600 --> 00:30:40,000
The other one of your books I keep close to hand and one of my favorite camping books is Camping's Top Secrets.

532
00:30:40,360 --> 00:30:41,399
I like that book too.

533
00:30:41,440 --> 00:30:42,000
That's a fun one.

534
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:48,980
It's in its third edition now and you keep revising and going over stuff, so it stays very relevant.

535
00:30:49,280 --> 00:30:51,660
You have a favorite tip or piece of advice from the book?

536
00:30:51,880 --> 00:30:53,120
Yeah, I guess I do,

537
00:30:53,700 --> 00:31:01,200
And it's one of those bits of advice that it's a constant battle with people because they refuse to acknowledge it,

538
00:31:01,680 --> 00:31:09,840
And it's this business of always put a plastic ground cloth inside your tent, not under the floor,

539
00:31:09,840 --> 00:31:13,800
And so I do battle with people all the time over this.

540
00:31:14,300 --> 00:31:20,860
I like to tell those of the folks out there blogging how floors got put into tents to begin with.

541
00:31:21,419 --> 00:31:27,580
Floors were not put into tents to keep stuff out of the tent, believe it or not.

542
00:31:28,020 --> 00:31:31,879
But you know, setting a tent up without a floor, you've got to know what you're doing,

543
00:31:32,480 --> 00:31:36,820
Otherwise you keep moving stakes around because it's a rectangle, an unsupported rectangle.

544
00:31:36,820 --> 00:31:44,000
So somebody got the idea to put a floor in a tent so it would just literally set the floor pattern so you could get the tent up easier.

545
00:31:44,200 --> 00:31:46,040
That's why floors were put in tents.

546
00:31:46,659 --> 00:31:54,020
The problem with the floor is any water that gets in the tent through seams in the floor stays inside the tent.

547
00:31:54,580 --> 00:31:58,840
So that's why you need a ground cloth inside the floor and not underneath it.

548
00:31:58,840 --> 00:32:05,439
But I would say that's the biggest, bestest tip that I can give anybody:

549
00:32:05,639 --> 00:32:08,899
Don't put a ground cloth underneath the floor of the tent.

550
00:32:09,220 --> 00:32:10,959
People think it protects the floor.

551
00:32:11,459 --> 00:32:12,720
Actually it doesn't,

552
00:32:13,240 --> 00:32:17,080
And I know all this is all heresy, I mean to a lot of people.

553
00:32:17,699 --> 00:32:20,240
You know, and the way we discovered this was very interesting.

554
00:32:20,820 --> 00:32:22,679
You know what a green stick break is.

555
00:32:22,680 --> 00:32:26,800
Well, you take a green stick between your hands and you bend it until it breaks. okay,

556
00:32:26,920 --> 00:32:28,480
The bottom compresses,

557
00:32:28,820 --> 00:32:30,360
The top elongates

558
00:32:30,540 --> 00:32:33,520
And then what happens is the top part snaps

559
00:32:33,820 --> 00:32:35,340
But the bottom doesn't.

560
00:32:35,840 --> 00:32:38,720
So a green stick always breaks from the top when it's bent.

561
00:32:39,140 --> 00:32:41,960
Well, that's the same thing that happens to the floor of a tent.

562
00:32:42,420 --> 00:32:44,700
The coating is on the inside of the floor,

563
00:32:44,700 --> 00:32:53,580
So when the stick starts poking up through the floor of the tent, what happens is the fabric stretches and the coating breaks at the top.

564
00:32:54,200 --> 00:32:59,040
If you put your ground cloth on the inside of the tent, it will prevent you from getting holes in the floor.

565
00:32:59,460 --> 00:33:05,200
And if you put holes in the floor and you have a ground cloth on the inside, it doesn't matter because you've got a ground cloth on the inside.

566
00:33:05,600 --> 00:33:06,040
Exactly,

567
00:33:06,200 --> 00:33:13,240
If you put the ground cloth under the floor, then any water that gets trapped between the ground cloth and the floor gets pressure wicked into the tent.

568
00:33:13,240 --> 00:33:15,620
So I'd say that's my favorite tip.

569
00:33:15,720 --> 00:33:18,700
I'm definitely with you on the ground cloth inside of the tent.

570
00:33:18,800 --> 00:33:27,560
As a matter of fact, believe it or not, you know my fallback if you didn't have a favorite tip, was to ask you about that one, because that's my favorite one.

571
00:33:28,820 --> 00:33:31,900
What I want you to do is you just tell your buddies once to do this

572
00:33:32,080 --> 00:33:33,580
And they won't do it.

573
00:33:33,580 --> 00:33:40,740
So what you do is, after you set up camp, that night you go out on the little point out there, look towards the setting sun and you look up into the sky and say,

574
00:33:41,180 --> 00:33:42,760
Oh God, please make it rain,

575
00:33:43,240 --> 00:33:43,680
Okay.

576
00:33:45,020 --> 00:33:51,420
And then if it rains that night and they get flooded out and they'll see that you're not, they won't say a word,

577
00:33:51,680 --> 00:33:54,000
They will not say anything to you at all.

578
00:33:54,120 --> 00:33:57,060
From that point on, they'll always put the ground cloth inside the tent.

579
00:33:57,780 --> 00:34:01,100
I mean, I'm almost on a crusade about that.

580
00:34:01,100 --> 00:34:09,339
Well, you know, when I wrote that article about putting the ground cloth inside the tent, one of the editors from Scouting Magazine called me up and said:

581
00:34:09,420 --> 00:34:10,779
You know, don't you have this backwards, Cliff?

582
00:34:10,900 --> 00:34:11,759
You know, is this an error?

583
00:34:12,159 --> 00:34:13,679
I said no, it's not an error.

584
00:34:13,679 --> 00:34:18,339
But, to their credit, they're willing to embrace new ideas

585
00:34:18,419 --> 00:34:19,259
And I think that's great.

586
00:34:19,399 --> 00:34:22,159
I saw this book maybe five or six years ago

587
00:34:22,580 --> 00:34:24,399
And it said Camping's Top Secrets

588
00:34:24,400 --> 00:34:29,639
And I think the subtitle is A Lexicon of Camping Tips. Only the Experts Know.

589
00:34:30,180 --> 00:34:33,060
And I've been camping and going out for a number of years

590
00:34:33,180 --> 00:34:35,120
And I thought, yeah, right,

591
00:34:35,240 --> 00:34:37,040
There's not going to be a whole lot in this,

592
00:34:37,100 --> 00:34:38,860
But I'll give the old guy a chance anyway,

593
00:34:38,980 --> 00:34:39,500
Oh, thanks,

594
00:34:39,500 --> 00:34:42,960
And there was a lot I didn't know,

595
00:34:43,719 --> 00:34:44,139
Thank you.

596
00:34:44,280 --> 00:34:45,940
So it's a worthy read.

597
00:34:46,179 --> 00:34:48,040
It's a fun book too,

598
00:34:48,280 --> 00:34:54,620
And then you can be converted to the sect of intent ground cloths

599
00:34:54,820 --> 00:34:56,860
And we'll trade stories back and forth,

600
00:34:56,980 --> 00:34:57,500
All right.

601
00:34:59,500 --> 00:35:04,280
What authors influenced your writing and the way that you approach the outdoors?

602
00:35:04,960 --> 00:35:08,940
Well, the number one total mentor was Calvin Ruttstrom.

603
00:35:09,580 --> 00:35:14,540
His books, by the way, are back in print through University of Minnesota Press

604
00:35:15,160 --> 00:35:17,360
And I would encourage folks to read them:

605
00:35:17,460 --> 00:35:26,240
North American Canoe Country, New Way of the Wilderness, Wilderness Route Finder and Paradise Below Zero.

606
00:35:26,380 --> 00:35:27,680
He wrote a whole slew of them

607
00:35:28,140 --> 00:35:29,700
And I just loved the guy,

608
00:35:29,700 --> 00:35:38,120
And actually his first book called The Way of the Wilderness. he was working for a Boy Scout camp, I think in Minnesota,

609
00:35:38,940 --> 00:35:42,380
And they asked him to write a pamphlet for the boys

610
00:35:42,980 --> 00:35:45,980
And so he wrote this pamphlet thing called The Way of the Wilderness

611
00:35:46,080 --> 00:35:50,840
And it was so popular that it became. he turned it into a book called The New Way of the Wilderness.

612
00:35:51,620 --> 00:35:57,540
Sig Olson, another very famous Minnesota writer. he was more of a descriptive writer.

613
00:35:57,540 --> 00:36:00,120
He would describe the shimmering fish in the moonlight,

614
00:36:00,120 --> 00:36:03,120
But Ruttstrom would tell you what hook and line to use to catch him.

615
00:36:03,620 --> 00:36:05,560
So you know I was really a Ruttstrom fan

616
00:36:05,740 --> 00:36:14,100
And he was really number one for me, followed by Horace Capehart who wrote Camping and Woodcraft in 1917.

617
00:36:14,600 --> 00:36:15,580
And you know it's kind of interesting.

618
00:36:15,580 --> 00:36:22,900
In 1917, Capehart had a little kit. you know his daily kit that he went on.

619
00:36:23,020 --> 00:36:24,600
I think he weighed like something like nine pounds.

620
00:36:25,100 --> 00:36:26,080
That was his total kit.

621
00:36:26,200 --> 00:36:27,660
I mean, you're talking about going light today,

622
00:36:27,759 --> 00:36:28,440
Give me a break.

623
00:36:28,819 --> 00:36:37,060
And one of the things that Capehart observed in 1917 was that insect head nets should be colored black.

624
00:36:37,279 --> 00:36:38,560
And the same thing on tents.

625
00:36:38,560 --> 00:36:45,500
Look out the screen of a tent with light colored netting and it reflects the lights right back into your eyes again.

626
00:36:46,100 --> 00:36:46,200
Right,

627
00:36:46,640 --> 00:36:49,140
With black colored netting, you can see clearly.

628
00:36:49,500 --> 00:36:53,640
So I tell people, well, just take a black magic marker and color the netting black.

629
00:36:53,779 --> 00:36:54,259
You won't believe it.

630
00:36:54,259 --> 00:36:55,420
Oh, that's not a bad idea,

631
00:36:55,540 --> 00:36:56,400
That's a great idea.

632
00:36:56,940 --> 00:36:57,120
Yeah,

633
00:36:57,400 --> 00:36:58,480
Yeah, and it works.

634
00:36:58,580 --> 00:37:02,160
I mean, just color the eye panel black and all of a sudden you'll see clearly.

635
00:37:02,160 --> 00:37:07,720
And another one also was a guy, but his pen name was Nesmeck.

636
00:37:07,819 --> 00:37:09,160
It was George Washington Sears.

637
00:37:09,279 --> 00:37:10,160
He lived out in the Adirondacks

638
00:37:11,140 --> 00:37:14,100
And he wrote a book called The Woodcraft and Camping in 1920.

639
00:37:14,299 --> 00:37:20,140
And what's interesting is Capehart's book was Camping and Woodcraft, and Nesmeck's was Woodcraft and Camping,

640
00:37:20,220 --> 00:37:23,879
So I guess they didn't, you know, care too much about titles and those.

641
00:37:24,020 --> 00:37:28,339
But those were really the three guys, the three old timers, that inspired me most.

642
00:37:28,600 --> 00:37:30,339
So what are you working on right now?

643
00:37:30,379 --> 00:37:31,399
Any new books in the works

644
00:37:31,400 --> 00:37:33,840
I have under revision.

645
00:37:34,140 --> 00:37:36,140
We're revising the Boundary Waters book.

646
00:37:36,720 --> 00:37:39,760
It's going to be a huge, major revision, going to full color.

647
00:37:39,840 --> 00:37:41,300
It'll be a very nice book,

648
00:37:42,400 --> 00:37:47,000
But it won't be out until next year or, I think, in the spring of 2012..

649
00:37:47,580 --> 00:37:51,960
But basically what I'm happy doing, I'm happy keeping my books in print.

650
00:37:52,320 --> 00:37:56,020
I really love writing for the Boy Scouts,

651
00:37:56,660 --> 00:37:59,340
But I don't want to chain myself to a computer anymore

652
00:37:59,340 --> 00:38:03,320
Now. as you know, I'm getting to the age now where I want to play.

653
00:38:03,680 --> 00:38:05,120
I don't want to look at a computer screen,

654
00:38:05,420 --> 00:38:08,840
But I still enjoy talking to groups and giving presentations and things like that.

655
00:38:08,860 --> 00:38:09,780
That's a lot of fun for me.

656
00:38:09,780 --> 00:38:11,680
You're headed off for the Rio Grande

657
00:38:12,140 --> 00:38:12,420
Yep,

658
00:38:12,560 --> 00:38:12,780
Yep

659
00:38:12,780 --> 00:38:13,680
In about a week here.

660
00:38:13,880 --> 00:38:14,600
That'll be great.

661
00:38:14,740 --> 00:38:17,440
Well, once again, I really appreciate your time.

662
00:38:17,560 --> 00:38:18,680
Thanks for talking with us

663
00:38:18,680 --> 00:38:21,879
And we hope to talk to you again sometime.

664
00:38:22,520 --> 00:38:22,899
Thanks, Clark,

665
00:38:23,020 --> 00:38:25,060
Take care of yourself and best to all your listeners.

